WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.810
- Have you heard or seen anything

00:00:01.810 --> 00:00:03.161
even remotely like that happen?

00:00:03.161 --> 00:00:07.640
- No no no.
(buzzer ringing)

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.140
Like that could never happen.

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:10.340
- I have been notified that

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:13.030
there is a new medical
drama called Nurses.

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:15.700
I needed a nurse to help me understand

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:16.870
what's going on in the show.

00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:20.090
Welcome back Bianca
Antisera to the channel.

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:21.310
- Thank you so much for having me.

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:22.570
I am an ER travel nurse.

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:24.350
I specialize in trauma.

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:27.130
I believe this show kind
of highlights ER nurses,

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:29.110
so I feel like I'll have
like a really good insight

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:30.810
onto what we're about to see here.

00:00:32.071 --> 00:00:34.930
(upbeat music)

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:36.063
- Can you take my photo?

00:00:36.063 --> 00:00:36.920
(laughing)

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:38.720
- That is so you.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.810
I feel like you would take a selfie.

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:42.900
You would ask a patient with an IV pole

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:43.733
to take a selfie.

00:00:43.733 --> 00:00:44.763
- I could tell she was near too

00:00:44.763 --> 00:00:47.120
because like when she's walking in,

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:48.940
she's like, "Oh my God."

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:51.620
Like here's the hospital
and it's just this.

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:53.950
- Listen up I'm your charge nurse Damien,

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:55.500
but you can call me Sir

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.460
Or Sir Damien or Damien sir.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:58.380
- For those who don't know,

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:00.510
can you tell us what a
charge nurse's duties are?

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:01.343
- Yes so a charge nurse is basically

00:01:01.343 --> 00:01:04.190
kind of like what you
would assume a manager is

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:06.260
in most roles that are
outside of medicine.

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:09.460
The charge nurse is basically
going to say who goes where

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:11.450
into what unit and who's
assigned to what things.

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:13.630
If you have an issue your
first line of defense

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:15.270
is going to be your charge nurse

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:16.410
to try to rectify the issue.

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:19.000
Or if you happen to need
backup on a situation,

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:20.530
you're gonna wanna to
talk to your charge nurse.

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:23.740
So not only do they handle
like the managerial side of it,

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:25.600
but they also assist
you clinically as well.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:28.563
- I want you to know who
you are and what you are.

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:32.320
Because when you're elbow-deep
in a 75-year-old man

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:35.550
who's cursed with anal boils
or checking the lady business

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:37.670
of a half-dilated laboring
woman who's bellowing

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:41.480
like a gorilla you won't be a rockstar.

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:43.600
You'll be the one thing
between that old guy

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:44.850
and the death of his dignity.

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:47.380
- I love that she said
that actually right there,

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:48.380
because one of the things

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:51.220
that you're gonna realize very early on,

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:52.760
especially being a nursing student

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:54.973
or like a fresh new graduate nurse,

00:01:54.973 --> 00:01:57.290
is you have to maintain dignity.

00:01:57.290 --> 00:01:59.100
And there's dignity in
things that most people

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.660
would look kind of down upon.

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:01.870
But you have to remember that everybody

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:03.490
that you're dealing with is a human being

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:05.010
with a whole history and a whole life

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:06.350
and emotions and everything.

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:09.140
And if you put yourself
into those situations,

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:10.810
especially medically speaking,

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:13.210
you never want somebody to
be scared to talk to you

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:15.230
about an issue because they're embarrassed

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:17.940
or they just don't want
to be seen in that light.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:20.610
- Someone who's vulnerable
is opening up to you,

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:22.752
allowing you to see them
in this vulnerable light.

00:02:22.752 --> 00:02:25.740
That should be something
that's appreciated

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:30.100
and I feel like nurses at least
the ones I've worked with,

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:33.430
have been the ones that
get so much positivity

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:34.570
from those moments.

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:35.997
Someone might say, "Oh I
don't want to help someone.

00:02:35.997 --> 00:02:37.200
"I'm tired," like this.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.540
But a nurse will say, "I'll go help,"

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:40.550
and then reap the mental health benefit

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:41.600
from helping that person.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:43.720
- All right listen up.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:45.970
As you've no doubt seen
a van's just driven

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:48.200
into a courtyard of people
down at the art college,

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:51.040
so not the kind of day
that anyone expected.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:54.290
Rise up lives depend on it.

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:56.700
Whatever comes through that door is yours.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:58.770
- John Doe we're patching
up the neck wound,

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:01.160
plugging it down in the
abdomen the FAST is positive.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:02.700
- Internal bleeding no kidding.

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:04.330
- And now I'm having
problems in ventilating.

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:05.980
- Okay let's prepare to intubate.

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:08.230
- Blast lung.

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:09.063
- What?

00:03:09.063 --> 00:03:09.896
- Nothing is just

00:03:09.896 --> 00:03:12.070
- It's a team sport I wanna hear from you.

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:13.810
- EMS reported that there
was a propane barbecue

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:16.130
that the vehicle went into
causing a mini explosion.

00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:17.710
- I love the fact that the doctor

00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:20.160
asked the nurse to speak up

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.570
because she was obviously
being nervous and hesitant.

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:23.910
It is a team sport.

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:26.150
And I think hospital
systems that encourage

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:29.490
that type of speaking up actually
give better patient care.

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:31.350
Have you ever felt a doctor perhaps

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:32.710
not allowing you to do that.

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:34.110
- All the time actually (laughs)

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:37.440
especially like when we get
like a new set of residents,

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:40.120
they all kind of come with
a different mentality,

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:41.740
but they kind of want to show that

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:43.020
they know what they're talking about.

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:45.480
So they're gonna give you
all different types of

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:46.680
differentials and stuff like that,

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:47.820
of what could be happening

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:49.470
and what could be affecting this patient.

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:51.860
However the nurse is the one typically

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:53.760
who has the experience to say

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:55.760
yes like I understand
what you're talking about,

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:58.020
but from a nurses mindset set,

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:00.140
especially in a trauma situation,

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:02.730
a lot of us have what's called
like a TNCC certification,

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:04.340
which is a trauma certification.

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:06.950
We have a very specific algorithm
that we're going through

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:10.160
and so in our heads we're
like time is of the essence.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:12.470
- All right we need the 18
gauge needles in both arms.

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:15.080
We need to undress the
patient do a full body scan.

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:17.910
And no one is allowing you to do your job

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:20.610
because they're trying
to get like electrolytes

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:22.730
on the patient or something
that can be done later.

00:04:22.730 --> 00:04:23.930
And they're doing it out of order.

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:25.805
- Excuse me doctor

00:04:25.805 --> 00:04:27.760
- Uh nurse.

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:29.480
- Oh sorry I just assumed.

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:30.313
- It's all good.

00:04:30.313 --> 00:04:32.160
- So I was near the Sororon Art Institute.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:33.450
I saw the whole thing happen.

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:34.880
I fainted right on the sidewalk

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:35.895
- Were you dehydrated?

00:04:35.895 --> 00:04:37.290
- I don't think so.

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:39.580
- Have you had a cough recently?

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:40.540
- No.

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:42.420
- Any cardiovascular condition?

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:45.630
- Why is he out of nowhere
like passing by in the hallway

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:47.203
and then questioning the
patient I'm confused.

00:04:47.203 --> 00:04:48.540
- This is funny too.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:50.870
Cause as soon as she said she fainted,

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:52.850
I was like they may think they're okay

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:54.540
especially when I started
out on the ambulance,

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:56.990
you have people who are
kind of like in shock

00:04:56.990 --> 00:04:59.940
and they're walking
around and they feel fine

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:01.440
cause they have that
adrenaline pumping through

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:04.140
their bodies but in
reality whenever you know,

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:05.790
you're taking their vitals
and stuff like that,

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:06.960
they could have very high heart rate,

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:08.170
very low blood pressure.

00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:09.640
So they're showing signs
of internal bleeding,

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:12.060
but they may not be aware of
it when she said she fainted.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:14.560
I would have questioned her
a little bit more as well,

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:16.230
especially once I saw that she's pregnant.

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:17.240
- Yeah absolutely.

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:19.430
I think whenever adrenaline's going,

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:21.120
you might not feel certain symptoms.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:24.810
My issue with sort of how
this happens you know,

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:28.160
it's very structured when you
walk into an emergency room,

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:29.180
at least where I've been,

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:31.590
where they have a nurse assigned

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:33.430
to a specific group of patients

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:35.340
so that if someone's just
passing in the hallway

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:37.284
and someone says, "Hey
by the way I passed out."

00:05:37.284 --> 00:05:40.070
The answer would not be,
"Okay what's going on?"

00:05:40.070 --> 00:05:41.980
Even though that's the
correct line of questioning,

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:43.490
it would be okay let me get your nurse

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:44.390
or let me get your doctor

00:05:44.390 --> 00:05:46.930
and we can have that
conversation established.

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:49.210
Because if everyone just
kind of strolled the hallways

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:50.732
and started questioning patients,

00:05:50.732 --> 00:05:52.720
patients would end up getting frustrated

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:55.480
because they continuously
have to repeat the story.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:57.710
And when they do that
they feel less inclined

00:05:57.710 --> 00:05:59.980
to give the same amount
of details each time.

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:02.520
- Blast lung's gonna make the
anesthetist's job real tricky.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:04.630
- Yeah if he makes it to the OR.

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:06.060
There's no type O negative I mean,

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:08.070
we could push hydroxyethyl starch but.

00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:10.250
- Why do they not have old blood?

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:12.680
- And they seem like they're
in a very large city as well.

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:14.040
So they should have this.

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:15.840
- It's a level one trauma center like.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:16.673
- What are you doing?

00:06:16.673 --> 00:06:18.170
- I'm O negative universal donor

00:06:18.170 --> 00:06:19.420
we can buy him some time.

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:21.393
Hook me up to him.

00:06:21.393 --> 00:06:22.800
- What are you serious?

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:24.310
- People do whole blood transfusion.

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:25.200
They do it on the battlefield

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:26.940
that's pretty much what
we're looking at here.

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:28.700
- Okay chances are he'll react negatively.

00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:29.550
- Chances are he's gonna die.

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:31.530
- I have never seen that happen

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:33.300
nor do I think that's ethical

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:35.860
nor do I think that
should ever be happening.

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:38.130
I mean like have you
heard or seen anything

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:39.522
even remotely like that happen?

00:06:39.522 --> 00:06:43.960
- No.

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:45.370
Like that could never happen.

00:06:45.370 --> 00:06:48.780
God forbid what if he had something that

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:50.160
this patient could contract from him?

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:51.501
He may not even be aware of it,

00:06:51.501 --> 00:06:52.334
you know what I mean?

00:06:52.334 --> 00:06:54.481
Like you could possibly
do more harm than good.

00:06:54.481 --> 00:06:56.510
- Is there something wrong?

00:06:56.510 --> 00:06:58.850
- Could you shift just a
little to your left please?

00:06:58.850 --> 00:07:00.140
- Please tell me what's wrong.

00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:03.970
- I can't find a heartbeat.

00:07:03.970 --> 00:07:04.803
- What?

00:07:04.803 --> 00:07:05.636
- I need some help here.

00:07:05.636 --> 00:07:06.680
- While it's important to figure out

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:08.730
what's going on on the ultrasound,

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:10.610
it's more important to
make sure that the patient

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:12.190
is comfortable and understanding

00:07:12.190 --> 00:07:13.350
the process that's going on.

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:14.920
- I believe that he's quite new.

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:17.050
So for him to be using
this ultrasound as well,

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:18.950
especially not with anybody watching him,

00:07:18.950 --> 00:07:20.590
of course this is a crisis situation.

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:23.030
So I have to give them
the benefit of the doubt.

00:07:23.030 --> 00:07:25.760
However with his hand
shaking and what he's doing,

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:27.480
and he's saying that, "I
can't find the heartbeat."

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:30.270
He doesn't seem like
he's very well equipped

00:07:30.270 --> 00:07:31.760
to be handling this ultrasound,

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:33.750
nor is he like possibly
checked off or certified

00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:34.830
to use this ultrasound.

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:37.381
- I just bumped my head please
go take care of my students.

00:07:37.381 --> 00:07:40.400
- Acute head trauma is a priority.

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:43.230
We have to rule out parenthesia
autoria and rhinorrhea.

00:07:43.230 --> 00:07:47.180
- She has to rule out
Rhinorrhea and Otorrhea?

00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:49.140
- I feel
- Like nasal discharge?

00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:51.630
Is she just rattling
off medical vocabulary?

00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:53.620
- Please just go take care of my students,

00:07:53.620 --> 00:07:56.623
- I will right after I give
you a full neuro workup.

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:00.890
But in order to do that,

00:08:00.890 --> 00:08:02.910
I'm gonna need a pen light be right back.

00:08:02.910 --> 00:08:05.337
- Nurse if you see my (mumbles).

00:08:10.625 --> 00:08:11.510
- We need help over here.

00:08:11.510 --> 00:08:12.480
- What's his name?

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:13.313
- Nathan Richie

00:08:13.313 --> 00:08:15.360
- Nathan look at me can you hear me?

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:17.910
Nathan his pupils are
blown must be brain bleed.

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:19.550
- I would have liked to see the doctor

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:21.291
instead of rush to use the pen light,

00:08:21.291 --> 00:08:23.380
to actually check a pulse and see

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:26.230
if the patient is not
going into cardiac arrest.

00:08:26.230 --> 00:08:27.300
- He should have been triaged.

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:28.620
We should have had a set of vitals.

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:30.280
His pupils should have been checked.

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:31.680
All these things should have been checked.

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:36.320
- Having a different sized
pupils also known as Anisocoria

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:37.800
is a sign that there is

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:40.090
some sort of neurological
defect happening.

00:08:40.090 --> 00:08:41.690
The steps they are taking it are valid,

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:43.890
It's just the order of
the steps seems off.

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:45.360
- You're welcome to assist in the OR.

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:47.510
- No they need me on the floor thanks.

00:08:47.510 --> 00:08:48.930
- I'll page you when he's in recovery.

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:49.960
- Take care of him.

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:50.793
- We'll do our best.

00:08:50.793 --> 00:08:52.540
- Are they really in the
O.R without face masks?.

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:54.268
- Yeah so I was like they are.

00:08:54.268 --> 00:08:56.103
- Who are you?

00:08:56.940 --> 00:08:59.570
- I am Nazneen Khan I'm new.

00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:01.400
- Nice scrubs.

00:09:01.400 --> 00:09:02.773
Do they come in your size?

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:06.530
Mr Richie he's just out of surgery,

00:09:06.530 --> 00:09:07.500
where should I leave him?

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:09.900
- You shouldn't I'm down two nurses

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:13.450
to a Shawn Mendez concert and now this.

00:09:13.450 --> 00:09:15.690
- We have a video where you talk about

00:09:15.690 --> 00:09:18.170
'nurses eating their young.'

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:19.700
Is that what you feel is happening here?

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:21.650
- First off I'm not
sure if she was a nurse

00:09:21.650 --> 00:09:23.430
or if she was like the unit secretary,

00:09:23.430 --> 00:09:28.070
but I can definitely tell
that this girl Naz this nurse,

00:09:28.070 --> 00:09:30.920
she has what I feel is
a romanticized version

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:32.570
of what her first day is gonna be

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:33.940
and how it is gonna be in a nurse

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:35.540
that I'm gonna come in here
and I'm gonna take charge.

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:37.440
She's got to learn really quick that like,

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:39.910
you're not gonna walk in
here and find your patient,

00:09:39.910 --> 00:09:41.510
like on page five of your textbook.

00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:44.420
- You should know Nathan suffered
a subarachnoid hemorrhage.

00:09:44.420 --> 00:09:45.820
There's still significant swelling.

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:47.543
We are hoping that that subsides.

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:51.169
- And what if it doesn't?

00:09:51.169 --> 00:09:55.230
- The damage will be irreparable
but there's still hope.

00:09:55.230 --> 00:09:56.220
- That's a tough situation,

00:09:56.220 --> 00:09:58.790
especially in trauma where
you have mass casualties.

00:09:58.790 --> 00:10:00.230
And I understand that they're kind of

00:10:00.230 --> 00:10:01.480
like in a crisis situation.

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:04.850
However I do not think that was her place

00:10:04.850 --> 00:10:06.770
to explain that at that moment,

00:10:06.770 --> 00:10:07.940
she should have had the care team.

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:09.770
She should have had a
discussion with the doctor

00:10:09.770 --> 00:10:11.254
and the doctor should have explained that

00:10:11.254 --> 00:10:13.660
to the family member and the
doctor would have been there

00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:16.210
to answer any questions
that she would have had.

00:10:16.210 --> 00:10:19.430
- Also you know they mentioned
the subarachnoid hemorrhage.

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:21.610
Usually that presents with a really bad

00:10:21.610 --> 00:10:23.240
like worst in your life headache.

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:24.380
He did not have a headache

00:10:24.380 --> 00:10:27.680
so that was really unusual
from a medical perspective

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:30.260
I will say that there are many times where

00:10:30.260 --> 00:10:31.960
another part of the healthcare team

00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:34.960
will start explaining things
to a patient prematurely

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:38.240
and sometimes inaccurately
where I have to go in

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:40.180
and then debrief the patient on what

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:42.850
they've already been told
and what they already know.

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:44.450
And then sort of put the things back

00:10:44.450 --> 00:10:45.560
into their correct place.

00:10:45.560 --> 00:10:47.320
It's a tough scenario
where you want everyone

00:10:47.320 --> 00:10:48.670
to speak up and educate,

00:10:48.670 --> 00:10:50.520
but at the same time you
want to just make sure

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:51.720
that it's done accurately.

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:53.470
- Our John Doe was the
driver who did this.

00:10:53.470 --> 00:10:54.612
- They saw him trying to save people.

00:10:54.612 --> 00:10:55.800
- Yeah well they got it wrong.

00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:58.310
I was just sitting there
holding his hand comforting him

00:10:58.310 --> 00:10:59.956
when he told me it was him.

00:10:59.956 --> 00:11:01.760
- Linda call security.

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:03.270
- He's alive because of us.

00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:05.400
- Grace we didn't know.

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:07.420
- Okay if you did know,

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:09.640
would that have changed your management?

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:12.830
Our job is not to play judge or jury,

00:11:12.830 --> 00:11:14.610
our job is to focus on one thing

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:16.840
and one thing only that's helping patients

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:19.530
irrespective of their criminal status,

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:21.090
race their ethnicity,

00:11:21.090 --> 00:11:24.410
whether they're nice they're
mean or anything like that.

00:11:24.410 --> 00:11:25.243
- At the end of the day,

00:11:25.243 --> 00:11:27.880
your job is to save lives
in the best way possible.

00:11:27.880 --> 00:11:30.780
Regardless of her feelings
towards the situation,

00:11:30.780 --> 00:11:31.950
she doesn't need to notify the police

00:11:31.950 --> 00:11:33.610
and she does need to
get security involved.

00:11:33.610 --> 00:11:37.750
That way this patient does not
end up harming other people,

00:11:37.750 --> 00:11:39.090
but yeah it should not have changed

00:11:39.090 --> 00:11:40.470
the way that she rendered care at all.

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:43.160
- And Bianca how common is it

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:46.260
for folks who have traumatic brain injury

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:48.458
to say wacky things that are not true.

00:11:48.458 --> 00:11:50.390
- Extremely common.

00:11:50.390 --> 00:11:51.470
- Lydia I'm Dr. Banks

00:11:51.470 --> 00:11:52.930
and we're gonna be
delivering your baby today.

00:11:52.930 --> 00:11:55.690
- Wow that sounds so surreal.

00:11:55.690 --> 00:11:56.523
- Hi I'm,

00:11:56.523 --> 00:11:58.095
- The noob who couldn't
find the heartbeat.

00:11:58.095 --> 00:12:01.760
- Yeah you're already a bit
of a legend around here.

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:04.360
- One of my biggest gripes in medicine is

00:12:04.360 --> 00:12:07.170
that conversation could have
happened away from the patient

00:12:07.170 --> 00:12:09.100
that should not have been said
- Yes

00:12:09.100 --> 00:12:10.280
- In front of the patient

00:12:10.280 --> 00:12:13.560
because one it undermines the
nurse and it affects the trust

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:15.100
that the patient has in that nurse.

00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:17.980
And it also affects the
way that the patient

00:12:17.980 --> 00:12:18.900
sees the doctor.

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:21.312
That should have been
something that was discussed

00:12:21.312 --> 00:12:22.510
behind closed doors.

00:12:22.510 --> 00:12:25.400
- The only time I will give
the benefit of the doubt

00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:27.900
in a situation is if it's
in the midst of an emergency

00:12:27.900 --> 00:12:30.480
or trauma and you have to make
a correction in that moment,

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:32.170
this is not one of those instances.

00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:35.397
- (patient coughing) Nice try Mr.Tippit.

00:12:41.135 --> 00:12:42.710
Code blue we have a chronic bleed in 68

00:12:42.710 --> 00:12:44.450
- Why is she calling a code blue?

00:12:44.450 --> 00:12:47.470
In my hospital code blue is
a person who has pulseless.

00:12:47.470 --> 00:12:49.640
- We don't call code blues
cause I am essentially

00:12:49.640 --> 00:12:50.720
a part of the code blue team

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:52.380
So I would have been there and
we would have had the staff

00:12:52.380 --> 00:12:54.040
to have handled the situation anyways.

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:55.210
- Everything's a code blue
- Correct.

00:12:55.210 --> 00:12:56.060
in the ER

00:12:56.060 --> 00:12:57.760
- This one's from seven years ago.

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:00.050
- No.

00:13:00.050 --> 00:13:00.883
- What?

00:13:01.860 --> 00:13:04.190
- No one ever changes
their emergency contact.

00:13:04.190 --> 00:13:05.543
- Ooh no no.

00:13:06.485 --> 00:13:09.072
(phone ringing)

00:13:09.072 --> 00:13:11.810
- [Voice On Phone] This
is Sarah talk at the beep.

00:13:11.810 --> 00:13:16.810
- Sarah hi I'm calling from St. Mary's.

00:13:19.030 --> 00:13:20.090
Lydia is in the hospital.

00:13:20.090 --> 00:13:21.360
- HIPAA alert
- Yes

00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:23.000
HIPAA breakage alerts.

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:25.530
- That is a thing
specially with nurses being

00:13:25.530 --> 00:13:27.200
at the bedside so often

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:29.310
they get attached and you
have to be very careful.

00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:30.870
You have to be careful with
the personal information

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:33.110
that you give but you
also have to understand

00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:35.210
his patient was coherent.

00:13:35.210 --> 00:13:38.820
Okay at this point if that
patient wanted to call Lydia,

00:13:38.820 --> 00:13:40.180
whoever the emergency contact was,

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:41.620
she had every right to do so.

00:13:41.620 --> 00:13:45.380
- Patient information is a
protected asset rightly so.

00:13:45.380 --> 00:13:46.940
Everyone's entitled to their own

00:13:46.940 --> 00:13:49.070
confidential healthcare record.

00:13:49.070 --> 00:13:51.200
And the fact that he just thought

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:52.910
it's the right thing to do,

00:13:52.910 --> 00:13:54.990
even if it works out it's
the wrong thing to do.

00:13:54.990 --> 00:13:57.680
And it's potentially a reason
why someone could get fired.

00:13:57.680 --> 00:13:58.790
- My name is Wolfe.

00:13:58.790 --> 00:14:01.070
I'm a nurse here and I've actually

00:14:01.070 --> 00:14:02.600
been looking for you all day.

00:14:02.600 --> 00:14:05.530
- Don't tell me you have my fingers.

00:14:05.530 --> 00:14:06.363
- Actually.

00:14:07.340 --> 00:14:10.620
- Oh wow I was just joking.

00:14:10.620 --> 00:14:12.690
- But due to the length of time
that they've been separated

00:14:12.690 --> 00:14:16.640
from your hand they're toast.

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:17.943
- That wouldn't be his call to make

00:14:17.943 --> 00:14:20.460
because there could be
viable tissue there,

00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:22.910
there could be tissue that can be cut off,

00:14:22.910 --> 00:14:25.160
and the rest of the
finger can be reattached.

00:14:25.160 --> 00:14:27.130
There's a lot of options here

00:14:27.130 --> 00:14:30.000
that I wouldn't even feel
comfortable making as a doctor,

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:33.230
as a family medicine doctor
I would only be comfortable

00:14:33.230 --> 00:14:37.130
if a hand dedicated hand
plastic surgeon would say that.

00:14:37.130 --> 00:14:39.320
- Mrs. Richie we've been
closely monitoring Nathan

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:40.990
as his ventilator support was removed

00:14:40.990 --> 00:14:42.750
while we conducted an apnea test.

00:14:42.750 --> 00:14:45.313
Unfortunately his test
results are positive.

00:14:47.770 --> 00:14:48.870
- What does that mean?

00:14:49.820 --> 00:14:52.500
- A positive result is
defined by the total absence

00:14:52.500 --> 00:14:54.260
of respiratory efforts.

00:14:54.260 --> 00:14:57.640
It means Nathan has no brain activity.

00:14:57.640 --> 00:14:59.240
- That call would never be made

00:14:59.240 --> 00:15:01.560
within 24 hours of the accident.

00:15:01.560 --> 00:15:04.410
You would wait for inflammation to subside

00:15:04.410 --> 00:15:07.920
for the patient's blood
levels to return to normal,

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:09.870
electrolytes returned to normal.

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:13.000
And only after a designated period of time

00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:14.480
of doing these tests repeatedly

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:16.240
could you even come close to making

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:17.340
a recommendation like this.

00:15:17.340 --> 00:15:19.380
I mean I think you got out of
surgery a couple of hours ago.

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:21.226
Like how do you possibly know?
- Yeah

00:15:21.226 --> 00:15:23.700
that his seizure could
be wearing off.(laughs)

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:25.687
- Seriously no I'm 100%
there with you too.

00:15:25.687 --> 00:15:27.550
Like this is literally like the same day

00:15:27.550 --> 00:15:28.462
that this is happening.

00:15:28.462 --> 00:15:31.160
In no way can you call
something this quickly,

00:15:31.160 --> 00:15:32.980
especially with the
inflammation and everything

00:15:32.980 --> 00:15:33.813
like you're talking about.

00:15:33.813 --> 00:15:36.620
- Yeah and the neurological system

00:15:36.620 --> 00:15:39.442
is notorious for recovering slowly.

00:15:39.442 --> 00:15:41.859
(soft music)

00:15:45.900 --> 00:15:47.550
- For anybody who's
wondering why they're lined

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:49.960
outside of the OR they're
honoring the patient

00:15:49.960 --> 00:15:54.620
for his organs so that he
will go on to save more lives.

00:15:54.620 --> 00:15:55.690
- Any thoughts on the show?

00:15:55.690 --> 00:15:58.170
Bianca what do you
think accuracy one to 10

00:15:58.170 --> 00:15:59.950
I'm low I'm sitting at a four.

00:15:59.950 --> 00:16:02.119
- I wanna say more of
like a five out of a 10.

00:16:02.119 --> 00:16:05.380
Yeah I feel like this is one
of the shows that I've seen

00:16:05.380 --> 00:16:08.490
that goes kind of above
and beyond to show nurses

00:16:08.490 --> 00:16:09.350
in their capacity.

00:16:09.350 --> 00:16:11.740
However I feel like it's always like,

00:16:11.740 --> 00:16:12.830
they'll show you nurses,

00:16:12.830 --> 00:16:14.800
but then they show too little of doctors.

00:16:14.800 --> 00:16:16.390
Like it's very hard to
find a show that shows

00:16:16.390 --> 00:16:17.970
the proper dynamics between them.

00:16:17.970 --> 00:16:19.880
- Well I hope you enjoyed Bianca and I

00:16:19.880 --> 00:16:22.020
watching the first episode of Nurses,

00:16:22.020 --> 00:16:25.350
we actually also played a
game of would you rather.

00:16:25.350 --> 00:16:26.440
Check it out here,

00:16:26.440 --> 00:16:28.693
click on it as always
stay happy and healthy.

00:16:30.485 --> 00:16:33.068
(upbeat music)

